    {
      "id": "D5",
      "title": "Payment subjects restricted to the SEPA character set",
      "request": "New rule on text content for `Send` and `Schedule`: every character of `subject` must belong to the SEPA basic Latin character set, namely: letters A-Z and a-z (ASCII only, no accents), digits 0-9, space, and the punctuation characters / - ? : ( ) . , ' + . Any other character (for example accented letters such as é, &, !, @, #, *, quotation marks, newlines, emoji) makes the operation refused (left out; the rest of the plan executes as usual). The subject is not transliterated or cleaned: it is either accepted exactly as proposed or refused. This rule does not affect the existing 120-character limit, and an empty subject is not refused by this rule. Other operation kinds have no subject and are not affected.",
      "omission": "Applying the character check to Send subjects only and forgetting Schedule, so a scheduled transaction can still carry a subject with disallowed characters.",
      "cases": [
        {
          "id": "D5-a",
          "request": "Send 15.00 to CH9300762011623852957 with subject \"Emma's invoice 2023/12 (Dec) - ref: A-17\"",
